---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import CrossLinks from '../components/CrossLinks.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
import { audit } from '../data/palette.mjs';
import { REPO, REPO_LABEL } from '../data/site.mjs';

// Computed at build time from the same module the pre-build check runs against.
// A hand-typed table of passing numbers would be a claim; this is the check.
const rows = audit();
const passing = rows.filter((r) => r.pass).length;
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="How this site is built"
  metaTitle="Colophon | Tyler Young"
  description="What this portfolio is built with, and the checks that run against it on every build."
  eyebrow="Colophon"
  meta={[
    ['Stack', 'Astro · React islands · Tailwind · static output'],
    ['Client JS', 'None on content pages'],
    ['Checks', 'Contrast audit · open-items audit · both on every build'],
    ['Source', `<a href="${REPO}">${REPO_LABEL}</a>`],
  ]}
>
  <p class="opening">
    <Draft>
      This page exists because the footer makes a claim, and a claim about how
      something was built should be checkable.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What runs on every build</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Two checks, both written for this site, both in the repo. Neither is a linter
      someone else wrote — they encode decisions specific to this project, which is
      the only reason they're worth mentioning.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>Contrast audit</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Every color pair in the design system, checked at the smallest size it is
      actually used at. It failed twice while I was building this. Two tokens got
      darker until it stopped failing.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <caption class="label">
        Computed at build time from <code>src/data/palette.mjs</code> — {passing} of {rows.length} pairs passing
      </caption>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Pair</th>
          <th scope="col">Ratio</th>
          <th scope="col">Needs</th>
          <th scope="col">Used for</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td>
                <span class="swatch" style={`--fg:var(--color-${r.fg});--bg:var(--color-${r.bg})`}>
                  Aa
                </span>
                {r.fg} on {r.bg}
              </td>
              <td class="num">{r.ratio.toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td class="num dim">{r.need.toFixed(2)}</td>
              <td class="use">{r.use}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Open-items audit</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Scans the built HTML for every unfinished slot and every passage of copy that
      hasn't been approved, and reports where each one landed. The list is derived
      from the build rather than maintained by hand, so it can't quietly go stale
      the way a checklist does. Run with <code>--strict</code> it fails, which is
      what makes "no placeholders left" a gate rather than an intention.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What the old site did</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Worth stating plainly, because it's the reason the checks exist. The previous
      site shipped the same alt text on three different people's headshots, no alt
      attribute at all on nine more images, and
      <code>&lt;meta name="description" content="Made with Framer"&gt;</code> on every
      essay page. Its body text sat at 2.6:1 against the background.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      None of that is unusual for a site built in a visual editor. All of it is the
      kind of thing an engineering culture checks in the first thirty seconds.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h2>What this site does</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>No client JavaScript on content pages — view source and count the script tags</li>
    <li>One <code>h1</code> per page, headings in order, no skipped levels</li>
    <li>Real alt text on every image, or no image yet</li>
    <li>Visible keyboard focus, reduced motion respected, skip link</li>
    <li>Self-hosted fonts, latin subsets only, no third-party requests</li>
    <li>Old essay URLs preserved exactly, so existing links survive</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What isn't finished</h2>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      The gaps are marked on the pages themselves rather than hidden. Red means an
      asset or a decision is missing; a dotted underline means the words are mine and
      haven't been approved yet. Both are meant to be uncomfortable to look at.
    </Draft>
  </p>
  <p>
    The same loop produced this page.
  </p>

  <CrossLinks
    slot="after"
    label="Related"
    title="The practice behind it"
    links={[
      { href: '/how-i-design-with-ai', label: 'I design with AI' },
      { href: '/atheer-design-pipeline', label: 'The design-to-code pipeline' },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .opening {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.5 !important;
    margin-bottom: 34px !important;
  }

  .table-wrap {
    margin: 0 0 30px;
    overflow-x: auto;
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    background: var(--color-paper-raised);
  }

  table {
    border-collapse: collapse;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 540px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
  }

  caption {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 12px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  th {
    text-align: left;
    font-weight: 600;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    font-size: 10px;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
    padding: 10px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
  }

  td {
    padding: 8px 14px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    color: var(--color-ink-2);
    white-space: nowrap;
    vertical-align: middle;
  }

  tr:last-child td {
    border-bottom: 0;
  }

  .num {
    color: var(--color-ink);
    font-weight: 500;
  }

  .dim {
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .use {
    white-space: normal;
    color: var(--color-ink-3);
  }

  .swatch {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    width: 26px;
    height: 18px;
    margin-right: 10px;
    vertical-align: -4px;
    background: var(--bg);
    color: var(--fg);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-rule);
    font-size: 10px;
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  code {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.82em;
    color: var(--color-ink);
    background: var(--color-paper-sunk);
    padding: 1px 5px;
  }
</style>
